From 93c59613421c8f9465e26652e7bb2a84964f8d75 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 19:53:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] What the company makes: sixty products, one drawn per
 table
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Every game started a hardware line named after the seat id, and told the
player "Hardware". The four kinds have always had genuinely different
economics and no story attached to any of them. This is the story.

One product is drawn from the game's own seed and given to everybody at
the table, so each group in a cohort faces the same market — and so that
four companies competing over one pool of buyers makes sense, which it
would not if they were each selling something different.

A second line is a VARIANT of that product rather than an unrelated one.
The round resolver pools every line from every company into a single
sharedDemands call: a firm running two lines competes with itself. Naming
the second line "bike helmets" next to "espresso grinders" would promise a
second market that does not exist, and the player would meet the truth as
their own two lines ate each other. As "a commercial grinder" every
mechanic reads correctly and cannibalisation becomes the lesson.

Leaving your own kind stays available and stays priced: newLine() already
carries over 60% of accumulated know-how instead of 100%, and the launch
card now says so instead of letting it be discovered.

p.name is untouched. It is the key every decision, demand split and order
is filed under; p.display is cosmetic and nothing computes with it. There
is a test asserting the two never converge.

The kind is pinned to hardware unless a host asks for another. Letting the
draw pick an economy failed season.mjs immediately, and the cause was not a
bug: every number in this project — the contract's 1.04, the lease rates,
the level comparison, the tournament format — was measured against a
hardware start. Varying it is one config flag away and is a change to
measure, not one to slip in behind a naming exercise.

Also restores src/live-template.html, which had fallen behind the deployed
page and was missing the entire recruiter screen. public/live.html was
committed; the template it was built from was not. The next mklive.py run
would have rebuilt the site from the stale source and silently deleted the
hiring screen. Reconstructed from the deployed page and verified by
round-trip: rebuilding from this template reproduces the previously
deployed live.html byte for byte.

Tests: 26 of 28 pass. accounts.mjs and billing.mjs fail identically on an
untouched clone of this repo and are not from this change.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W5Cgk3o25Ekv1uiVwpSQQk
