From 0825d385780576bb7852b97c837e18bf1cb44fbb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 22:31:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Filing a round on a phone: a tall slider and the forecast
 underneath
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Six controls a line, each a slider sharing 390px with its number box. Price
runs in 50c steps across 1.1x the product's value — about 220 stops in the
230px the slider gets, so one pixel is one step and nobody can aim it. R&D
and advertising move in $2,500 and $1,000 and were always fine; price and
production were the problem.

Below 620px the slider row is replaced by a 56px tap row that opens a sheet
with the same control turned on its side: a 455px track and a 56px thumb,
2.3x the resolution. The forecast stays pinned underneath and moves as the
thumb does, so the price that makes the most money is found by thumb rather
than by arithmetic — the profit curve has an interior peak, and that is the
central lesson of the economy made physical.

The sheet is a second SKIN on the same <input type=range>, not a second
control. It writes to that input and dispatches `input`, which is the event
the orders form and the projection were already listening for. One value,
one code path, and the desktop keeps its native keyboard and screen reader
behaviour untouched. A test asserts the range, the number box, the tap row,
the sheet and D.orders all agree after a drag.

Two things that would have been quiet bugs:

The sheet resolves its input by (product, field) every time rather than
holding the node. A poll can redraw the orders form mid-drag, and a captured
node would become an orphan that swallows every subsequent gesture while
looking fine. render() repaints the sheet against the replacement, and a
test drives a redraw mid-adjust and then checks a step still moves the real
value — stepping DOWN, because the drag before it leaves price at its
maximum where "+" cannot move and "unchanged" would pass for the wrong
reason.

The vertical slider is built by hand. -webkit-appearance:slider-vertical is
deprecated, the writing-mode route disagrees across browsers about which end
is "more", and neither lets a marker be drawn on the track. It carries the
slider role, min/max/now, and the arrow keys.

Price gets a dashed marker at what the product is worth to a buyer, and the
factory one at today's plant. Above or below the market becomes a glance.

Also fixes three tests that waited for a range input to be VISIBLE. Four run
at desktop width and were unaffected; mobileui (390px) and tourneyui's
entrants (420px) now wait for it to be attached instead. The inputs are
deliberately hidden on a phone, so those waits could only ever time out.

Tests: node suite passes but for accounts.mjs and billing.mjs, which fail
identically on an untouched clone. All 16 browser tests pass, including a
new adjustui.mjs.

